CREATED:20261029T191706Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>Constraining the growth of massive black holes wit
 h a population-level multi-messenger approach</b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract: JWST’
 s discovery of a large population of active black holes at z > 5\, and the 
 evidence from Pulsar Timing Arrays (PTAs) for a gravitational-wave backgrou
 nd likely produced by supermassive black hole binaries\, offer new constrai
 nts on the growth and evolution of massive black holes. Both observations c
 hallenge standard expectations. JWST points to more numerous or more rapidl
 y growing black holes at earlier times than many models predict\, while the
  estimated PTA amplitude seems to require a larger-than-expected population
  of very massive black holes at low redshift. Meanwhile\, tidal disruption 
 events (TDEs) open a window onto the elusive population of intermediate-mas
 s black holes. In this talk\, I will present L-GalaxiesBH\, a semi-analytic
  model that follows the formation\, growth and dynamics of massive black ho
 les and their host galaxies from the first seeds to the local Universe. I w
 ill then show how we jointly confront it with JWST\, PTA and TDE data to co
 nstrain the model’s parameters and piece together the evolutionary history 
 of massive black holes.</p>\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261029T191707Z
LOCATION:MP 1318A
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Silvia Bonoli (Donostia International Physics Center)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
