X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:It Takes Time: producing theatre with The Howland Company
DTSTART:20261102T233000Z
DTEND:20261103T010000Z
DTSTAMP:20261020T140800Z
UID:MEC-3aca25419b5a8e3ac42e17f49c9ef75f@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261102T233000Z
SEQUENCE:4
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/
 10/theatre.jpg
CATEGORIES:School of the Arts, Media, Performance &amp\; Design
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261020
DESCRIPTION:Join us for wide ranging conversation with The Howland Company
  ( https://howlandcompanytheatre.com/ ) Founding Members Ruth Goodwin\, Ca
 m Laurie and Courtney Ch’ng Lancaster\, as they discuss the production h
 istory of Casimir and Caroline and the gifts and challenges of running a t
 heatre company with your friends.\nDate: Monday November 2\, 2026\nTime: 6
 :30 pm\nLocation: Joseph G. Green Theatre\nFree Admission\nThe Howland Com
 pany was founded 13 years ago as a vehicle for ten emerging theatre artist
 s to create work for themselves. Since its inception\, the company has pro
 duced nearly 20 shows at various sizes and scales included the world premi
 ere of Casimir and Caroline\, first collectively workshopped in 2015 befor
 e premiering in January 2020.\nMost recently The Howland Company co-produc
 ed Lucy Kirkwood’s The Welkin with Soulpepper and Crows’ Theatre (nomi
 nated for 5 2026 Dora Awards)\, produced Susanna Fournier’s take rimbaud
 \, a new work first commissioned in 2013 (nominated for 5 2026 Dora Awards
 )\, and were selected for the 2026 Best of Fringe with YOU CHOOSE: An Impr
 ovised Murder Mystery.\nWe will also share a special sneak peek screening 
 of THAT WHICH IS HEARD\, an intimate documentary short that follows actres
 s Shruti Kothari\, whose Dora Award–winning performance in Howland’s C
 asimir and Caroline captivated audiences in January 2020. Just weeks after
  the show closed — and only days before the pandemic shut down theatres 
 worldwide — Shruti’s life changed dramatically when she suffered a str
 oke at the age of 30. Her road to recovery and a return to the stage is ch
 ronicled in this documentary short directed by Ken Pham and produced by Ru
 th Goodwin\, Kirthiga Rajanayagam and Shruti Kothari.\nPARTICIPATING ARTIS
 TS\nRuth Goodwin is a Toronto-based actor\, writer\, producer and director
 . She is best known for her roles on Global TV’s Private Eyes and Netfli
 x’s Wedding Season\, alongside appearances on Baroness Von Sketch\, Work
 in’ Moms and Murdoch Mysteries and more. She also recently starred oppos
 ite Academy Award Winner Adrien Brody in a Super Bowl commercial ranked am
 ong the year’s Top 10 by The New York Times.\nRuth is a founding artist 
 of Toronto’s critically acclaimed Howland Company\, where her work has e
 arned her numerous Dora Mavor Moore Award nominations\, Best of Fringe Awa
 rds and a Toronto Theatre Critics Award. She also received a Canadian Come
 dy Award nomination for her improvised stage show Entrances and Exits\, al
 ongside Colin Mochrie\, Andrew Phung and Elizabeth Johnston. She has been 
 named a “Top Artist to Watch” by the Toronto Star and NOW Magazine.\nB
 eyond acting\, Ruth spent six years as a Segment Producer for CTV’s Cana
 da AM before producing the short film Stuck\, which has amassed over 50 mi
 llion views on Amazon Prime. She made her film directorial debut with The 
 Strangers’ Case\, currently streaming on CBC Gem.\nCameron Laurie is a T
 oronto based actor and theatre producer. He is a Founder and Artistic Lead
 er of The Howland Company\, where he has performed in a number of shows in
 cluding The Welkin\, Heroes of the Fourth Turning\, Prodigal\, Three Siste
 rs\, Punk Rock and Casimir & Caroline\, for which he won a Dora Mavor Moor
 e Award for Outstanding Ensemble. Other theatre credits include Saving Gra
 celand\, The Trials of Maggie Pollock\, The Donnelly’s Trilogy\, The Dra
 wer Boy\, Our Beautiful Sons (Blyth Festival)\, A Little Closer (The Vault
  Creation Lab)\, The Merchant of Venice (Shakespeare Bash’d).\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261020
LOCATION:Joe G. Green Theatre\, Centre for Film and Theatre @ York Univers
 ity\, 4700 Keele Street\, Toronto\, ON
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/it-takes-time-producing-theatre-with-th
 e-howland-company/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
