SUMMARY:Snap Circuits for Kids
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T170000
UID:6aa445f9101eb7003f2f53b8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:For children ages\n8 to 12\n.\nLight up your imagination! In t
 his hands-on workshop\, young engineers will explore the basics of electri
 city and electronics using Snap Circuits. We'll learn how to build simple 
 circuits to power lights\, make sounds\, and more. Participants will work 
 in pairs to construct projects from the Snap Circuits blueprints. No prior
  experience is needed—just bring your curiosity!\nDrop in. No registrati
 on required\, but space is limited.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa445f9101eb7003f2f53b8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — School Age
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 School Age": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac43b9d377296003a49f9d5"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_school_age
SUMMARY:Magical Kitties Save the Day
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T170000
UID:6ac43b9d377296003a49f9d5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:People are mysteriously disappearing in River City and their f
 ellow humans don't seem to notice or miss them. Where could they be going 
 and what could have happened to them? It might have been your human\, or t
 he human of a kitty you know\, but the only clues you have point to the ma
 in branch of the River City library. Can you find out what’s going on\, 
 and more importantly can you rescue the missing humans?\nMagical Kitties S
 ave the Day is a role-playing game for players 6 and up in which you the p
 layer assume the role of a magically endowed feline who secretly looks aft
 er your human as well as the larger community. Nobody knows about your mag
 ical powers\, and you need to keep it that way!\n*For children ages 6-12. 
 Registration is required.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac43b9d377296003a49f9d5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — School Age
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 School Age": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa18e6aa5dbde002aa30000"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_school_age
SUMMARY:Let's Make a Graphic Novel! Workshop with Tory Woollcott
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T170000
UID:6aa18e6aa5dbde002aa30000@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Led by the professional cartoonist\, Tory Woollcott\, this wo
 rkshop teaches the fundamentals of story outlining and narrative structure
  as it applies to comics & graphic novels\, prose\, or even video games! D
 rop in\, limited spots.\nTory Woollcott is a Toronto-based author\, illust
 rator\, designer\, and educator whose work spans graphic novels\, science 
 comics\, audio dramas\, podcasts\, and kids' books. She is the author of S
 cience Comics: The Brain: The Ultimate Thinking Machine (First Second Book
 s)\, a vibrant and accessible exploration of neuroscience\, illustrated by
  Alex Graudins. Her debut graphic novel\, Mirror Mind\, is a celebrated a
 utobiography that recounts her experiences growing up with dyslexia. Other
  notable works include the audio drama Awake with Sparklermonthly\, the tr
 avel diary Toronto to Tuscany (created in collaboration with Kean Soo)\, a
 nd two successful Kickstarter campaigns: the Tessera Oracle and its follow
 -up\, the Trine Oracle.
LOCATION:High Park\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa18e6aa5dbde002aa30000
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — School Age
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 School Age": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad7bb5ba76c9f3e2ae8c886"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_school_age
SUMMARY:Discovery Club: Improv Olympics
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T170000
UID:6ad7bb5ba76c9f3e2ae8c886@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:For ages 9 to 12.\nGet ready to laugh\, create\, and compete i
 n the Improv Olympics! Through fun theatre games\, storytelling challenges
 \, and team events\, participants will build confidence\, creativity\, and
  teamwork skills. Earn points for your team\, tackle hilarious improv chal
 lenges\, and discover the joy of thinking on your feet. No acting experien
 ce required!\nThis is a 6-week program and participants are recommended to
  attend all 6 sessions.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal info
 rmation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the 
 Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information
  will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalu
 ation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be dir
 ected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yon
 ge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Lower level program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad7bb5ba76c9f3e2ae8c886
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — School Age
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 School Age": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a91b84f1c197d11325b77f5"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_school_age
SUMMARY:After School Club: Diorama Art with Soyeon Kim
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T170000
UID:6a91b84f1c197d11325b77f5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn how artist/illustrator Soyeon Kim brings a world to life
  in her diorama art. Learn about this amazing art form and how you can sta
 rt making your own diorama at home. Soyeon will share how she uses drawing
 \, colouring\, and cutting techniques to assemble layered scenes that show
  depth\, texture\, and imagination. Then provide some inspiration for anyo
 ne ready to create their own world!\nRecommended materials:\nrecycled card
 board box\, white paper (letter size)\, drawing tools (pencil\, eraser)\
