BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abc0fdb377296003a4874e7"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Bee-ing a Pollinator Protector: Plan Now for a Buzzing Spring Gard
 en
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T200000
UID:6abc0fdb377296003a4874e7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Saving the bees is an environmental cause that resonates deepl
 y with Canadians.  While most of us might not be thinking about bees this 
 time of year\, our actions during the colder months can still support thes
 e pollinators\, particularly native bees\, which are crucial and threatene
 d by climate change\, habitat loss\, and other pressures.\nIn honour of th
 e late bee scientist Sheila Colla\, this presentation will offer practical
  tips on planting and maintaining gardens that provide beautifully bloomin
 g habitat for pollinators\, with a particular focus on how native plants s
 upport wild bees.\nPresented by\nLorraine Johnson\n\, who co-authored\nA G
 arden for the Rusty-Patched Bumblebee\nwith Sheila Colla\, as part of TPL'
 s environmental program series\,\nOur Fragile Planet\n\, supported by Frie
 nds of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nThis program is also pr
 esented as part of Climate Action Week.\nRegistration:\nThis virtual even
 t is free to join and will take place on\nZoom\n.\nRegister to receive an 
 email with details on how to join the session. Join live\, as there will n
 ot be a recording for this event.\nNeed help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and pa
 rticipating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZoom Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or 
 borrow from Toronto Public Library:\nA Garden for the Rusty-patched Bumble
 bee\n(book)\nA Garden for the Rusty-Patched Bumblebee\n(ebook)\n---\nThis 
 program will also be offered in person on Monday\, November 23 at the Riv
 erdale Branch. To register for the online program and for more info\, see 
 the listing [COMING SOON].\n---\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe perso
 nal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library un
 der the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The inf
 ormation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery a
 nd evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information ca
 n be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\,
  789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abc0fdb377296003a4874e7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abeca7311bbf300684ab1f0"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Creating your own Google Maps
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T200000
UID:6abeca7311bbf300684ab1f0@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Your neighbourhood tells a story and the data to prove it alre
 ady exists. In this three-session series\, you will learn to find real civ
 ic data about your community\, plot it on an interactive map\, and turn wh
 at you discover into a powerful advocacy piece. No technical experience ne
 eded. Just curiosity about where you live and a desire to make it better.\
 nArrive 30 minutes before or stay 30 minutes after the program for one-on-
 one consultations.\nThis program is part of TPL's Innovator in Residence s
 eries focused on civic innovation. From October 5 to November 28\, join pr
 ograms\, workshops and one-on-one consultations led by technology strategi
 st Ismail Mohamud to learn how technology can help you create positive cha
 nge in your community. Learn more at\ntpl.ca/iir\n.\nInformation Collectio
 n Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toron
 to Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\
 , and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regis
 tration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles
  your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.c
 a\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Second Floor\, Digital Innovation Hub
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abeca7311bbf300684ab1f0
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"YOHOMO": "https://www.yohomo.ca/event/protecting-sobriety-through-loss"}
X-SOURCE-ID:yohomo
SUMMARY:Protecting Sobriety Through Loss
DTSTART:20261103T190000
DTEND:20261103T190000
UID:a5a05846b34391fd2d41ad0fc800cd91@yohomo.ca
DESCRIPTION:Queer Death Salon is a virtual community space for all 2SLGBTQ
 IA+ people to come together to discuss death\, dying and grief. In her lif
 e Jackie Clark has seen the overlapping power of queer and recovery commun
 ities. Getting sober after a devastating loss and staying sober through an
 other was one of the hardest things She&#x27\;s ever experienced\, and it 
 is only through deep connections with other people and a lot of truth tell
 ing that She managed. This session is for 2SLGBTQIA+ people in recovery (h
 owever they may define it)\, struggling with substance use\, or supporting
  friends and family through the same\, to connect about how death and grie
 f intersect with addiction and recovery. Participants can expect some fram
 ing\, a grounding meditation\, and some prompts for peer-sharing. Particip
 ate as much as you’re comfortable (just listening is okay too)! *A note 
 about language and perspective: I\, Jackie\, will facilitate with deep car
 e and a concerted effort to use language as inclusively as possible. I als
 o understand that everyone’s experiences with both addiction/substance u
 se disorders and with grief are extremely individual. I am not an expert\,
  but a peer with lived experience. * Please feel free to bring your own ex
 perience to the table\, and also leave behind any perspectives that do not
  resonate with or serve you. Jackie Clark (she/they) is a queer person fro
 m Battersea\, Ontario celebrating six years of sobriety. In her profession
 al life she works for a non-profit and on a farm –part of that work is a
 dvocating for and building community among queer and trans farmers. Outsid
 e of work she fills her days with fostering animals\, running in the woods
 \, and spending time with friends. After the loss of her best friend in 20
 19\, she worked towards harm reduction and then addiction recovery\, event
 ually spending a year facilitating meetings for folks recovering from all 
 types of substance use and behavioral addictions. She believes firmly in t
 he wisdom and power of queer community to hold each other safely as we nav
 igate grief. ‍
LOCATION:Online\, online
URL:https://www.yohomo.ca/event/protecting-sobriety-through-loss
X-SOURCE:YOHOMO
END:VEVENT
