BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-ID:gcal_4h7dspldpuviiv4o1slj9o5p5c
X-SOURCE:CITA Seminars
DTSTART:20261104T204600Z
DTEND:20261104T214600Z
DTSTAMP:20261031T052732Z
UID:72qvb5ohs09can0k4pja2mahc4@google.com
CREATED:20261030T194726Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>How Orbital Eccentricity Erases Snowball Bistabili
 ty on Earth-like Planets </b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract: For the same stellar flux
 \, Earth's climate can be either warm and partly ice-covered\, or fully fro
 zen in a "Snowball" state. This bistability comes from ice-albedo feedback:
  once ice forms\, it reflects more sunlight and keeps itself frozen. A stab
 le Snowball episode requires this bistability. Snowball episodes on Earth a
 re linked to rises in atmospheric oxygen and in the complexity of life. I w
 ill start from the simplest global-mean energy balance model and show how i
 ce-albedo feedback produces bifurcations and bistability. I will then turn 
 to eccentric orbits. There\, 3D global climate model simulations show the b
 istability disappears at moderate eccentricity. On the blackboard I will ad
 d ice thermodynamics to the energy balance model to explain why. This sugge
 sts Snowball episodes may be rarer on exoplanets than previously thought.</
 p>\n<p>Host: <b>Soumendra Kishore Roy</b></p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20261030T194726Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Xuan Ji (CITA)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
