X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/seminar-series-2026-2027-dr-alex-speed-dalhousie-university/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:Seminar Series 2026 – 2027 – Dr. Alex Speed (Dalhousie Univers
 ity)
DTSTART:20261106T190000Z
DTEND:20261106T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20261024T053800Z
UID:MEC-eba9940b6dcffe49a14de9a4899ab466@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261106T190000Z
SEQUENCE:3
CATEGORIES:Faculty of Science,FSc Chemistry,Graduate Program in Chemistry
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261024
DESCRIPTION:Abstract\nThe Speed group works at the boundaries of main grou
 p and organic chemistry\, developing new catalysts\, primarily based on ph
 osphorus (III)\, and phosphorus (II)/(III) cycles\, for useful chemical tr
 ansformations\, including asymmetric reduction. This talk will encompass o
 ur efforts to functionalize the potent greenhouse gas sulfur hexafluoride 
 (SF6)\, and the unplanned yet fruitful discovery of carbon-carbon bond for
 ming reactions during these explorations. Beginning with initial reactions
  of SF6 with stoichiometric quantities of phosphines and phosphides\, mech
 anistic studies into the role of the phosphines revealed an unexpected rol
 e for phosphorus compounds in the unusual P(II) oxidation state. A further
  serendipitous discovery led to the development of new chemistry for the f
 ormation of carbon-phosphorus\, and ultimately carbon-carbon bonds. These 
 reactions\, including intermolecular alkene hydro(hetero)arylation reactio
 ns\, were made catalytic in phosphorus. The insights gained from this cata
 lysis were then reapplied to the challenging problem of catalytic function
 alization of SF6.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261026
LOCATION:140 Campus Walk
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/seminar-series-2026-2027-dr-alex-speed-
 dalhousie-university/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
