SUMMARY:Doubles: A Panel Talk About the Film\, Diversity & Indo-Caribbean 
 Voices
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T160000
UID:6ad78fc3e06996003a84bc1e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an extraordinary evening with the actors and produ
 ction team behind the six-time\, award-nominated film\nDoubles\n\, where d
 iversity meets imagination\, identity meets history and rarely heard Indo-
 Caribbean voices come together to celebrate the power of storytelling.\nTh
 e Canadian drama film\, Doubles\, set in both Trinidad and Toronto\, explo
 res the relationship between a son and his estranged father. When tragedy 
 strikes\, Dhani\, a Doubles stand operator dreaming of owning his own rest
 aurant\, must leave Trinidad to find his father\, whom he hasn't seen in y
 ears. Their reunion brings deeply held feelings to the surface as unexpect
 ed revelations change their lives forever.\nPresented by the\nCanadian Car
 ibbean Indian Cultural Collective (CCICC)\n\, this panel will feature:\nMa
 rk Sirju\n\, Producer\nErrol Sitahal\n\, Actor portraying Ragbir\nPremika 
 Leo\n\, Actor portraying Skye\nKenneth Mohabir\n\, comedian\, actor\, and 
 producer\, will moderate the discussion.\nThe film is approximately 1 hour
  29 minutes long\, followed by a discussion with the panelists about th
 e importance of Indo-Caribbean representation\, behind-the-scenes stories 
 about the film\, and an audience Q&A.\nFree program. Please register. Door
 s open at 1:45 pm. At 2:00 pm\, seating becomes first-come\, first-served 
 until capacity is reached\, with light refreshments (while supplies last).
 \nOrganization Info:\nThe\nCanadian Caribbean Indian Cultural Collective (
 CCICC)\nis a community organization representing Indians (currently referr
 ed to as South Asians) of various ethnicities\, regions\, and faiths\, who
  are the descendants of the indentured labourers taken by the British to t
 heir colonies in the Caribbean and around the world. Their mission is to s
 hare their stories of struggle\, resilience and contributions across the C
 aribbean and here in Canada.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal
  information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under
  the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The inform
 ation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and 
 evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can b
 e directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 78
 9 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad78fc3e06996003a84bc1e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
