BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad63569ebad220045465c5f"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:Discussing Treaties with Rick Monture
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T193000
UID:6ad63569ebad220045465c5f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join Professor Rick Monture for a discussion about pre-contac
 t peace agreements between Indigenous nations\, treaties between Indigenou
 s peoples and Europeans\, and the various "numbered" treaties in Canada. L
 earn about the philosophies behind the Dish With One Spoon wampum and its 
 significance today\, as well as the 1784 Haldimand Proclamation as it pert
 ains to First Nations in Southern Ontario. Come discover why treaties are
  a vital part of the movement towards Truth and Reconciliation!\nNo regist
 ration required.\nBio:\nRick Monture is a member of the Mohawk nation\, Tu
 rtle clan\, from Six Nations of the Grand River Territory.  He is a Profes
 sor in the Department of English & Cultural Studies and Indigenous Studies
  at McMaster University where he teaches classes on Indigenous literature\
 , Haudenosaunee history\, traditional narratives\, and Bob Dylan.  He is a
 lso a Community Research Fellow with Six Nations Polytechnic and the Deyoh
 ahage / Indigenous Knowledge Centre based at Six Nations and is an active 
 member of the language revitalization movement in his community.  In partn
 ership with the Woodland Cultural Centre in Brantford\, Rick currently hol
 ds a Social Science and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC) awar
 d for a project that examines the events that led to the overthrow of the 
 traditional Haudenosaunee government\, by Ottawa\, to implement an elected
  council system in 1924.  His book\,\nWe Share our Matters: Two Centuries 
 of Writing and Resistance at Six Nations of the Grand River\n\, was publis
 hed in 2014 and is an examination of the Grand River Haudenosaunee’s ass
 ertions of sovereignty and social justice through letters\, political acti
 vism\, literature\, and film. Rick lives at Six Nations with his family.
LOCATION:Spadina Road
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad63569ebad220045465c5f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ae37f6e0b801373af917242"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:The Way We Were: Seniors’ Memories of Life in the Junction
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T200000
UID:6ae37f6e0b801373af917242@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Get a glimpse of the Junction in past decades through the memo
 ries and family stories of people who were there. Back Lane Studios\, base
 d in Roncesvalles\, presents a selection of short videos from Mapping Our 
 Memories\, a project collecting place-based stories from older people in v
 ideo and written formats. You'll learn about:\nSheila McIlraith's talented
  mother\, a lively young woman who served as public health nurse at Annett
 e and Runnymede public schools\, drove her own car in the 1920s and was ch
 oir master and organist at High Park Baptist Church. But she gave all that
  up when she got married in her late 30s.\nLocal grocer Doug Breakwell\, w
 hose store was at the corner of Annette and Indian Grove Cres. He helped o
 ut Lorne Clayton's mother many times when she couldn't pay for groceries. 
 But the tables turned. In the 1950s\, when small stores were losing out to
  supermarkets\, Lorne was able to help Doug out.\nLois Broad\, who made ou
 r most popular video\nGrowing up in the Junction\,\nhad many vivid memorie
 s to share\, from the Minnies swimming pools at the corner of Quebec Ave. 
 and Bloor\, to High Park Avenue United Church\, where her parents were the
  first couple to be married.\nWe'll screen a curated selection of short do
 cumentaries\, learn about Mapping Our Memories\, and meet some of the peop
 le behind the stories during a Q&A.\nLight refreshments will be served. 
  Drop-in. No registration required.
LOCATION:Annette Street\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ae37f6e0b801373af917242
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
